# PingOne Privilege MCPGW — FRONT-END OIDC configuration
# Copy to pingone.env and fill in values.
# Mounted into the container at /var/lib/procyon/config/pingone.env, and also loaded
# as environment variables via env_file in docker-compose.yml.
#
# THIS IS THE REAL FRONT-END CONFIG — NOT legacy, NOT deprecated.
#
# PingOne is the OAuth server. The gateway is an MCP server sitting in front of ours.
# These are the credentials it uses to authenticate CLIENTS: client calls the gateway,
# gets a 401, is sent to PingOne to sign in, returns with a token the gateway accepts.
#
# Do not confuse it with the BACKEND hop (gateway -> our mcp-server). That is
# configured on the MCP Application in the Privilege console — MCP Server URL, Auth
# Mode, Auth Token — and is deliberately unauthenticated here (Static Token, empty
# value), because mcp-server runs MCP_AUTH_DISABLED=true.
#
# Open issue (2026-08-09): with this file correct and present, the gateway still
# answers 401 with no WWW-Authenticate. cyonproxy contains no reference to this
# filename or any of these key names, and the same failure reproduces on Ping's own
# hosted frontend. The consumer appears to be the guest agent — which reads the same
# settings under different names from procyon-guest-agent.env and registers them with
# the control plane — and that binary ships only with the native installer, not in the
# Docker image. See docs/PRIVILEGE-MCP.md §2026-08-09.
#
# An earlier revision of ping-mcpgw/README.md labelled this template the "legacy OIDC
# approach". That was wrong: it came from proving cyonproxy does not read the file,
# which says nothing about whether the approach is current.

# Public URL of this MCPGW — the nginx front door, NOT the proxy's -listen port.
# This is what the 401 challenge tells clients to visit, so it must be reachable
# from a browser. Redirect URI to register in PingOne: ${SERVER_URL}/callback
#
# Local Docker Compose (mcpgw-nginx publishes host 443):
SERVER_URL=https://mcpgw.local.ping-devops.com
# On the SE cluster instead use:
# SERVER_URL=https://mcpgw.ai-demo.ping-devops.com

# OIDC application credentials. Environment 8d4d7a4c is the target: it is the only
# environment we hold PingOne Privilege console access in, and the console is where
# the gateway cluster, the MCP Server application's Frontend Name, and the policy
# are authored. No console access means no way to configure the gateway at all.
#
# The gateway signs users in against THIS environment, so its users are the ones who
# reach the MCP tools: cmuir+ssoEndUser@pingone.com and cmuir+ssoAdmin@pingone.com.
# The banking demo's own users live in 01d89b06 and are unrelated to this hop.
# Known app here: deff60f5-5a67-4a6e-b283-47252856c89c.
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=<client-id>
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=<client-secret>

# PingOne authorization server endpoints.
OIDC_AUTH_URL=https://auth.pingone.com/8d4d7a4c-de40-4f71-9b98-0c3507cd4d1b/as/authorize
OIDC_TOKEN_URL=https://auth.pingone.com/8d4d7a4c-de40-4f71-9b98-0c3507cd4d1b/as/token
OIDC_USER_URL=https://auth.pingone.com/8d4d7a4c-de40-4f71-9b98-0c3507cd4d1b/as/userinfo

OIDC_SCOPES=openid profile email
